  <h2>What Happens Inside an Activewear Factory?</h2>
  <p>An activewear factory turns a product specification into repeatable garments. The work begins before sewing: the team must confirm the tech pack, measurements, fabric, trims, logo method, size grading and approved sample. Bulk production should start only when these inputs agree.</p>
  <p>For buyers, the important question is not whether a factory owns many machines. It is whether each production stage has a clear standard, responsible team and inspection record. This process control determines whether the bulk order matches the approved sample.</p>

  <h2>1. Product Review and Pre-Production Approval</h2>
  <p>The merchandising, pattern, sample and production teams review the order before material is released. They check construction details, measurement tolerances, color, logo placement, labels, packaging and the planned production sequence.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Confirm the latest tech pack and measurement chart.</li>
    <li>Approve the pre-production sample and size grading.</li>
    <li>Confirm fabric lot, trims, labels and packaging availability.</li>
    <li>Record buyer comments so production and QC use the same reference.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Fabric and Trim Inspection</h2>
  <p>Activewear fabric is checked before cutting because defects discovered later affect many finished pieces. Typical checks include color against the approved standard, usable width, GSM direction, visible defects, stretch behavior and quantity. Performance programs may also require shrinkage, colorfastness, pilling or recovery tests.</p>
  <p>Learn more in the <a href="/blogs/fabrics">activewear fabric selection guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>3. Pattern, Marker and Cutting Control</h2>
  <p>Approved patterns are converted into cutting markers that reduce fabric waste while respecting grain and stretch direction. The cutting team controls ply alignment, panel quantity, notches and bundle identification. Inaccurate cutting can shift seams, pockets and measurements even when sewing quality is good.</p>

  <h2>4. Sewing and In-Line Quality Checks</h2>
  <p>Bundles move through operations such as overlock, flatlock, coverstitch, elastic application, binding and bartacking. An in-line inspector checks early output for seam appearance, skipped stitches, measurement trends and construction errors. Correcting a repeated issue after the first bundles is safer than waiting until the full order is complete.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Check seam type and stitch density against the approved sample.</li>
    <li>Measure key points during production, not only at final inspection.</li>
    <li>Inspect stress areas such as waistbands, gussets, straps and pockets.</li>
    <li>Separate defects and record the corrective action before reinspection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Logo, Label and Branding Application</h2>
  <p>Heat transfer, silicone print, screen print, embroidery and other logo methods require different temperature, pressure, placement and durability controls. Labels and care content must match the approved artwork and destination requirements. Buyers should approve a complete branded sample before this stage.</p>

  <h2>6. Final Inspection and Packing</h2>
  <p>Final inspection checks workmanship, measurements, quantity, color and packing against the purchase order and approved sample. The inspection method may use an agreed AQL level or another written defect standard. Packing checks include size stickers, polybags, hangtags, assortment, carton marks and carton quantity.</p>
  <p>See the detailed <a href="/blogs/quality-control">activewear quality control guide</a> for buyer checkpoints.</p>

  <h2>What Buyers Should Request From the Factory</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A production timeline separating material, cutting, sewing, branding, inspection and packing.</li>
    <li>Photos or records from risk-sensitive production stages.</li>
    <li>Measurement and workmanship results linked to the approved sample.</li>
    <li>Final quantity, packing list and inspection status before shipment release.</li>
  </ul>

